/*
 * VEGA Aries V2 -- Unified Worker Safety Node
 * DHT11 + MQ135 + SOS button/buzzer + ADXL345 fall detection +
 * MAX30100 HR/SpO2 (manual FIFO) + OLED paging display
 * ---> E220-900T22D (LoRa) ---> ESP32 bridge ---> dashboard
 *
 * Merged from two separate sketches:
 *   1) DHT11+MQ135+SOS-button/buzzer LoRa sender (UART1, GPIO4/A0/5/6)
 *   2) ADXL345 fall detection + MAX30100 HR/SpO2 + OLED display
 *      (I2C0/J10 shared bus, GPIO9/13)
 *
 * WIRING MAP (combined, no pin conflicts):
 *   DHT11 DATA   -> GPIO4  (J2 pin 14)
 *   MQ135 AOUT   -> A0     (J10 pin 2)
 *   SOS Buzzer   -> GPIO5  (J2 pin 12)   -- manual SOS, button-toggled
 *   SOS Button   -> GPIO6  (J2 pin 10, INPUT_PULLUP, other leg to GND)
 *   OLED Button  -> GPIO9  (INPUT_PULLUP, other leg to GND) -- pages screens
 *   Fall Alarm   -> GPIO13 -- separate from the SOS buzzer, auto-triggered
 *   ADXL345, MAX30100, OLED all share I2C0 (J10): SDA->J10 pin10,
 *     SCL->J10 pin12, with 4.7k pull-ups to 3.3V
 *   E220 LoRa    -> Aries UART1 (RX1/TX1), M0/M1 -> GND
 *
 * CHANGES MADE WHILE MERGING (flagged explicitly, not silent):
 *
 * 1. Debug Serial baud unified to 115200 (the OLED/sensor file's rate) --
 *    the DHT11/MQ135/SOS file used 9600. This only affects your USB
 *    Serial Monitor setting, not any sensor or the LoRa UART (which stays
 *    a separate, fixed 9600 regardless).
 *
 * 2. FALL packet field now reflects the REAL fall-alarm state
 *    (FALL:1 while alarmActive, FALL:0 otherwise) instead of the
 *    hardcoded "FALL:0" placeholder from the original LoRa sender.
 *
 * 3. HR/SPO2 fields are only included in the packet when a finger is
 *    detected AND at least one valid sample has been averaged --
 *    otherwise they're omitted entirely (not sent as 0), because 0 bpm
 *    would falsely trip the dashboard's low-heart-rate CRITICAL alarm.
 *    Same reasoning as the earlier DHT11+MQ135+MAX30100 merge.
 *
 * 4. NEITHER the ADXL345 nor the MAX30100 halts the board forever if it
 *    fails to initialize (the OLED file's original MAX30100 failure did
 *    via an infinite while(1)). Given what we found in the Aries core's
 *    own Wire.cpp -- every I2C wait is an unbounded while() loop with NO
 *    timeout -- repeatedly calling into a sensor that never ACKed is a
 *    real hang risk, not just wasted time. So each sensor gets an
 *    explicit ID check at startup (ADXL345: DEVID register == 0xE5,
 *    MAX30100: getPartId() as before); if a sensor fails that check, its
 *    *ok flag goes false and the rest of the loop never calls into it
 *    again -- other telemetry (temp/humidity/gas/SOS) keeps flowing
 *    either way.
 */

